{
  "_about": [
    "Golden table for sealed attachment objects: the chunked PSA2 format and its padded version 3.",
    "Normative: docs/sealed-messages.md §Chunked objects (PSA2).",
    "Padding: docs/sealed-messages.md §Padded objects (version 3).",
    "No plaintext is written out. A plaintext of n bytes is the rule `byte i = (i * 7 + 13) mod 251` for i from 0 to n - 1, which every implementation builds itself. Keys and nonce prefixes are given in base64.",
    "`chunkSize`: the chunk-size rule alone, as {plaintextSize, chunkSize, chunkCount, objectSize}, so it can state a four-gigabyte object in one line.",
    "`vectors`: seal-and-open round trips. Reproduce `object` byte for byte from the rule plaintext, `key`, `noncePrefix` and `chunkSize`; open it back; agree with `digest` and every entry of `ranges`; and open each range on its own into the right slice of the plaintext.",
    "`tamper`: take the named vector's object, apply `mutation`, and require the verdict `expect`.",
    "Verdicts: `unreadable` (the object MUST NOT open, not even partly), `tooNew` (a PSA2 header of a later version: say an update is needed, never treat it as corrupt and never open it) and `notPsa2` (not a PSA2 object; a reader falls back to the version 1 one-shot format).",
    "Mutations on the object bytes: `swapChunks {a, b}` exchanges two full chunks, `dropChunk {index}` removes one, `truncate {bytes}` keeps the first `bytes`, `flipByte {at}` xors 0xFF into one byte, `setByte {at, value}` sets one byte, and `setU32 {at, value}` overwrites a big-endian u32.",
    "`paddedSize`: the bucket rule alone. `chunkSize` and `objectSize` there are computed from the padded size, since the chunk size is in the clear header.",
    "`paddedVectors`: round trips. Pad the rule plaintext of `plaintextSize` bytes to `paddedSize` with zero bytes, reproduce `object` byte for byte, and open it back to exactly `plaintextSize` bytes matching `digest`.",
    "`paddedTamper`: self-contained objects a version 3 reader MUST refuse, given the `size` and `digest` a pointer states. A padding byte that is not zero is refused even though every tag verifies."
  ],
